WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.240
It is easy to forget that a country so rich
in History like Italy, is – as a Nation

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.200
– younger that the US.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:11.809
Italy as we know it today became a unified
State only in 1861, following decades of struggle.

00:00:11.809 --> 00:00:16.690
At the forefront of many of those fights,
you would be sure to find today’s protagonist,

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:17.699
General Giuseppe Garibaldi.

00:00:17.699 --> 00:00:21.240
[click on link for pronunciation]
General Garibaldi cut a distinctive figure

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:27.170
amongst the protagonists of that epic novel
that is European history of the XIX century,

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:32.560
when Nations were forged in iron, blood, but
also in gold and deception.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:38.610
With his enviable beard, his Tuscan cigars,
Argentine ponchos, his trusty saber and six-shooter,

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:42.040
Garibaldi had become one of the most popular
men in the World.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:46.030
So much so that his men accepted his autographed
photos as a salary!

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:50.530
Today we are going to look at his revolutionary
and military career, especially at this most

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:54.800
famous ‘Expedition of the Thousand’ which
led to the unification of Italy.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.900
This is also the story of a natural born soldier,
and a natural leader of soldiers who became

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:05.110
a General with little formal training, only
direct experience of fighting.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:08.540
To put it in his own words:
“The use of the sword I learnt by defending

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:12.220
my own head …
… and in giving my best endeavours to split

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:15.510
those of others!”

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:27.750
The Young Italy
Giuseppe Garibaldi was born on the 4th of

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:34.270
July 1807 in Nice, now in France, but back
then part of the Kingdom of Piedmont-Sardinia.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:37.940
This was one of the many States in which the
Italian peninsula had been divided into for

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:38.940
centuries.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:43.090
North Eastern Italy was part of the Austrian
Empire, which also controlled indirectly other

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:46.590
smaller Duchies from Tuscany to the Adriatic
coast.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:50.550
Central Italy was part of the State of the
Church, ruled by the Pope, and the South was

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:54.870
ruled by the Bourbon dynasty, tied both to
Austria and Spain.

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:59.210
Young Giuseppe’s family lived off the sea:
they were fishermen and coastal traders.

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:03.560
Garibaldi also had close ties with the sea,
spending most of his spare time swimming and

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:04.560
sailing.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.660
He enlisted as a merchant sailor at a very
young age and sailed across the Mediterranean

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:09.660
and the Black Sea.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.560
It was during a voyage to Russia that he became
acquainted with some members of the Young

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:14.580
Italy movement.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:19.670
The Young Italy was a revolutionary organisation,
with ties to secret societies, led by Republican

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:21.650
activist Giuseppe Mazzini [Click link for
pronunciation].

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:26.280
Mazzini had instigated or participated in
many attempts to overthrow the rulers of the

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.840
Italian governments and was now living in
exile in London.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.030
Garibaldi was fascinated with Mazzini’s
ideals and pledged allegiance to the Young

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:38.370
Italy and their affiliated secret organisation,
the ‘Carbonari’[pronunciation].

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:43.500
The aim of these societies was to unify the
Italian people under a single democratic republican

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:44.500
Government.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:48.540
In 1833, Garibaldi enlisted as an officer
in the Navy of Piedmont-Sardinia.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:52.790
The following year, instructed by Mazzini,
he instigated a mutiny, intended to spark

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:56.470
a wider revolution and install a Republic
in Piedmont.

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:58.380
The plan failed miserably.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:02.730
He barely escaped capture, crossed the border
with France and hid in Marseille for some

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:03.730
time.

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:08.780
Yet, according to rumours, on the day he left
he found the time to make love to three different

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:09.780
women.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:10.780
Which may be true.

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:15.160
Or may be an early indication that he was
very good at propping up his own image.

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:22.630
South American Adventures
Physical prowess aside, young Garibaldi had

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:23.800
a thirst for adventure.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:29.450
In 1836 he set sail for South America, and
like a Che Guevara one century earlier, he

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:32.360
looked for libertarian causes he could serve.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:36.810
He found one in Brazil, where he joined the
separatist republic of Rio Grande do Sul.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:39.020
How did he serve their cause?

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:40.340
As a pirate!

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:45.680
Or a corsair more precisely: his job was to
prey on Brazilian shipping to harass their

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.000
supply lines and support the Republic.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:52.660
During these adventures he fell in love with
a married woman, Anna Maria Ribeiro da Silva,

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:57.050
better known as Anita, who would remain by
his side even in battle.

00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:01.510
The war with Brazil was doomed to fail and
Garibaldi was discharged from Rio Grande in

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:02.510
1840.

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:06.959
He was compensated for his services with a
herd of cattle, which he drove to Montevideo,

00:04:06.959 --> 00:04:10.200
Uruguay, with Anita and their son Menotti.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:16.430
In 1842 he joined another war of liberation:
that of Uruguay against Juan Manuel de Rosas,

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:18.120
the dictator of Argentina.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:22.590
Initially he was put in charge of the Uruguayan
navy, but in 1843 Garibaldi took command of

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:27.169
a newly formed Italian Legion, composed of
exiles and libertarians like him.

00:04:27.169 --> 00:04:31.210
Looking for cheap cloth for their uniforms,
he stumbled upon a bargain: red shirts, used

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:32.550
by abattoir workers.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:37.110
From then on, his troops would wear the distinctive
tunics and would be known as Redshirts.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:40.490
Garibaldi’s fame grew, not only in the Americas,
but also in Europe.

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:46.139
This was cemented by the victories he achieved
in 1846, most notably at the Battle of Sant’Antonio.

00:04:46.139 --> 00:04:50.599
In this engagement he first adopted his trademark
tactic: he would establish a defensive line,

00:04:50.599 --> 00:04:54.990
absorb the impact of the enemy’s frontal
attack and then outmanoeuvre it by launching

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:59.270
a surprise counter-attack on its flanks, often
by means of a bayonet charge.

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:04.249
His South American experiences gave him invaluable
training in the techniques of guerrilla warfare

00:05:04.249 --> 00:05:07.240
that he would later use against traditional
European armies.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:12.409
He had now acquired the reputation of a professional
rebel, an indomitable activist for the causes

00:05:12.409 --> 00:05:15.379
of independence and republican rule.

00:05:15.379 --> 00:05:22.419
Back to Italy
In April of 1848, Garibaldi, Anita and their

00:05:22.419 --> 00:05:25.300
children sailed back to Italy on a ship called
‘Speranza’[pronunciation]

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.689
Or ‘Hope’ in Italian.

00:05:27.689 --> 00:05:31.110
Mazzini had invited him to return to Europe
to join another war.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:34.039
A popular revolt had ousted the Austrians
from Milan.

00:05:34.039 --> 00:05:38.180
The King of Piedmont, Charles-Albert, had
taken the occasion to declare war on Austria

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:40.029
to aid the Italian rebels.

00:05:40.029 --> 00:05:42.349
This was the First War of Italian Independence.

00:05:42.349 --> 00:05:46.979
Mazzini saw the occasion to ‘infiltrate’
the struggle to further his Republican agenda.

00:05:46.979 --> 00:05:51.510
Garibaldi entered the fight against the Austrians
with a force of irregular volunteers: despite

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:56.860
his victories, the Piedmontese army had been
defeated at Custoza and signed an armistice.

00:05:56.860 --> 00:06:01.509
Outnumbered, Garibaldi dissolved his units
and evaded capture by the Austrians by escaping

00:06:01.509 --> 00:06:02.509
to Switzerland.

00:06:02.509 --> 00:06:06.490
The opportunity for another fight presented
itself at the end of 1848, in Rome.

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:11.909
Pope Pious IX had gained fame as a rather
liberal ruler, giving hope to Italian patriots

00:06:11.909 --> 00:06:15.879
that he may be willing to lead a federation
of Italian states under him.

00:06:15.879 --> 00:06:21.089
But in 1848 he revoked any liberal reform
he had instituted in the State of the Church.

00:06:21.089 --> 00:06:26.509
Mazzini and the Young Italy then staged a
revolt which drove the Pope from Rome and

00:06:26.509 --> 00:06:27.949
installed a Republic.

00:06:27.949 --> 00:06:31.990
In February 1849 Garibaldi had joined the
Roman Republic and was ready to defend it

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:33.169
by any means.

00:06:33.169 --> 00:06:38.040
In April 1849, newly elected French President
Louis Napoleon (later Napoleon III), sent

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:44.099
an army to Rome to restore order, as a means
to gain the favour of the Catholic electorate.

00:06:44.099 --> 00:06:48.259
A contingent under General Picard attempted
to seize the Janiculum hill.

00:06:48.259 --> 00:06:52.210
They were expecting to be welcomed with open
arms by the God-fearing Romans.

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:54.340
Instead, they found Garibaldi.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:58.539
The General put up a stiff resistance, stalling
the advance of Picard.

00:06:58.539 --> 00:07:01.419
And then, he replicated his tactic of Sant’Antonio.

00:07:01.419 --> 00:07:07.529
The French were taken by surprise and retreated
hastily, leaving almost 300 dead and prisoners

00:07:07.529 --> 00:07:08.529
behind.

00:07:08.529 --> 00:07:12.349
In May he defeated an army sent by the King
of Naples, and in June he was leading the

00:07:12.349 --> 00:07:15.219
defense of Rome against another French siege.

00:07:15.219 --> 00:07:19.879
But there was no chance of holding the city,
and the republic collapsed.

00:07:19.879 --> 00:07:25.309
In July of 1849 Garibaldi and Anita were on
the run from the French and the Austrians.

00:07:25.309 --> 00:07:28.189
While in hiding, Anita contracted malaria
and died in Giuseppe’s arms.

00:07:28.189 --> 00:07:29.529
He barely had the time to mourn her, when
he had to go into exile again.

00:07:29.529 --> 00:07:33.999
This time Garibaldi first fled to Tangiers,
then New York, where for a time worked in

00:07:33.999 --> 00:07:38.219
a candle factory with Antonio Meucci [pronunciation],
arguably the inventor of the telephone.

00:07:38.219 --> 00:07:44.379
In 1852 and 1853, Garibaldi had returned to
a life of sailing: he was now a merchant ship

00:07:44.379 --> 00:07:49.439
captain, transporting guano – i.e. bird
or bat excrement – from Peru to China.

00:07:49.439 --> 00:07:52.229
Only in 1854 he was allowed to return to Italy.

00:07:52.229 --> 00:07:56.719
Having collected a family inheritance Garibaldi
was able to buy part of the small island of

00:07:56.719 --> 00:08:02.059
Caprera, off the Sardinian coast, which remained
his home for the rest of his life.

00:08:02.059 --> 00:08:09.379
Call of War
It was the 2nd of March 1859 and Garibaldi

00:08:09.379 --> 00:08:14.539
was milking a cow in his Caprera farm when
he received a surprise visitor: a Piedmontese

00:08:14.539 --> 00:08:19.120
officer, envoy of Prime Minister Camillo Benso,
Count of Cavour.

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:20.120
[Camillo Benso]
[Cavour]

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:24.379
Cavour was a skilled politician who had been
engineering big plans for the Kingdom of Piedmont.

00:08:24.379 --> 00:08:28.929
When the War of Crimea broke out in October
1853, he had convinced the King Victor Emmanuel

00:08:28.929 --> 00:08:32.190
II to join the allied side against the Russian
Empire.

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:36.720
Cavour’s only interest was to have a seat
at the peace negotiations, in March 1856.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:41.640
Cavour took the occasion to approach Napoleon
III and bring to his attention the plight

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:45.630
of Italian citizens under the rule of the
Austrians and their allies.

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:49.520
He completed his seduction of Napoleon III
with the help of his own cousin, the countess

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:54.570
Virginia of Castiglione, who actually, physically
seduced the lusty French Emperor, drawing

00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:56.510
him closer to the Italian cause.

00:08:56.510 --> 00:08:57.510
[Virginia as in the American State.

00:08:57.510 --> 00:08:58.510
Ca - steel -ee -awe - ne.

00:08:58.510 --> 00:08:59.510
Ca as in Car, Ne as in Ned]
Napoleon III and Victor Emmanuel II signed

00:08:59.510 --> 00:09:03.940
a secret treaty, by which France committed
to intervene in a war against the Austrians,

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:05.200
should they attack Piedmont.

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:10.300
Cavour was preparing a trap to lure Austria
into war and thought he could make good use

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:11.640
of Garibaldi.

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:15.980
Following the envoy, Garibaldi met Victor
Emmanuel II in Turin.

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:20.400
These two were polar opposites ideologically,
but they immediately clicked, bonding over

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:23.270
their shared love of hunting, combat and women.

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:28.490
The King offered Garibaldi a formal commission
as General in the Piedmontese army, in charge

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:32.650
of the “Hunters of the Alps”, a voluntary
corps specialised in mountain warfare.

00:09:32.650 --> 00:09:38.050
Now, Garibaldi, Cavour and the King may have
had common enemies, nut not common goals.

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:42.410
Garibaldi’s aim was the unification of Italy
– if possible a Republican Italy.

00:09:42.410 --> 00:09:47.330
Cavour’s plans were to extend the territory
and power of Piedmont and the ruling house

00:09:47.330 --> 00:09:48.330
of Savoy.

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:52.720
In the first months of 1859 Cavour set the
stage: he started amassing troops on the border

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:53.720
with the Austrian empire.

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:58.300
And he encouraged Italians under Austrian
rule to flee to Piedmont where they would

00:09:58.300 --> 00:09:59.970
join Garibaldi’s Hunters.

00:09:59.970 --> 00:10:05.710
On the 23rd of April, the Austrians fell into
the trap: they issued an ultimatum for de-mobilisation,

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:06.880
which was refused.

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:11.530
The Second War of Italian Independence was
declared and the joint Franco-Piedmontese

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:13.660
forces sprang into action.

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:18.590
Garibaldi and his 3000 volunteers were outnumbered
and outgunned, but he managed to score important

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:20.910
victories in the Lombard Alps.

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:25.370
In May he defeated a larger force at the Battle
of Varese, by using his signature tactic.

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:28.611
Over the following weeks he captured the cities
of Como, [Brescia] and [Bergamo].

00:10:28.611 --> 00:10:33.590
By July, he was about to march into the [Trentino]
region, when he received urgent orders to

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:36.170
halt the advance, and to return to Turin.

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:40.970
Unbeknownst to him, on the 11th of July Napoleon
III had agreed a secret armistice with Austrian

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:42.600
Emperor Franz Josef.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:46.380
Horrified by the huge loss of life at the
Battle of [Solferino], the French Emperor

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:48.060
wanted to cease hostilities.

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:52.430
When the Piedmontese were informed, they had
no choice but to stop fighting, as the Austrians

00:10:52.430 --> 00:10:54.590
would outnumber them more than 2 to 1.

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:59.850
The outcome of the war was positive to the
King and Cavour: they had expanded into Lombardy,

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:02.520
annexing Milan and other important industrial
areas.

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:06.150
At the same time, they had annexed other smaller
independent states in the North.

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:10.820
The Kingdom of Piedmont now controlled roughly
one third of Italian territory.

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:14.590
On the other hand, Garibaldi was deeply disappointed:
not only because of the secret armistice.

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:19.520
He had also found out that as compensation
for the French intervention, Cavour had ceded

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:21.660
the city of Nice to Napoleon III.

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:27.670
Paradoxically, the hometown of the champion
of Italian unification was now part of France.

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:34.490
The Expedition of the Thousand
Garibaldi had a further disappointment in

00:11:34.490 --> 00:11:37.450
January of 1860, on a more personal level.

00:11:37.450 --> 00:11:41.670
On the 24th he married his young fiancée,
Countess [Giuseppina] [Raimondi].

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:44.860
Immediately after the ceremony, the General
received a mysterious note.

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:49.040
Upon reading it, he snarled at the bride:
“I thought I had married a Countess, not

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:50.040
a whore!”

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:53.131
Apparently Giuseppina had been seeing other,
younger men, in the past months and was in

00:11:53.131 --> 00:11:54.430
fact five months pregnant.

00:11:54.430 --> 00:11:57.000
Garibaldi had the marriage immediately annulled.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:01.130
The General returned once more to his beloved
Caprera to lick his wounds, but news from

00:12:01.130 --> 00:12:05.040
Sicily would lead him to unsheathe his sabre
once more.

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:08.230
In April 1860 a revolt broke out in Palermo.

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:13.070
The Southern half of the Italian peninsula
was ruled by King Francis II of the house

00:12:13.070 --> 00:12:15.730
of Bourbon, with ties to Austria and Spain.

00:12:15.730 --> 00:12:19.860
The Bourbons had a history of repressing revolutionary
outbreaks, especially in Sicily.

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:23.910
They succeeded also in this occasion, but
the revolt had inspired Garibaldi to do the

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:24.910
unthinkable.

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:28.410
He raised a volunteer force with plans to
invade Sicily, cross the Messina straits to

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:33.350
the mainland and fight his way ‘up the boot’,
conquering Naples and perhaps even Rome.

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:36.740
This would mean finally unifying Italy into
a single state.

00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:41.160
At this stage he had realised that a Republican
Italy was a far-fetched dream and was content

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:44.100
with it being a monarchy under Victor Emmanuel
II.

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:48.430
On the night between the 5th and 6th of May
General Garibaldi launched the Expedition

00:12:48.430 --> 00:12:53.920
of the Thousand, which would cement his status
as a legendary icon in Europe and America.

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:59.880
With a force of roughly 1000 volunteers, aged
11 to 69, Garibaldi ‘borrowed’ two steamers

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:01.170
and set sail for Sicily.

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:05.630
The official version of the story goes that
Victor Emmanuel II and Cavour, who outwardly

00:13:05.630 --> 00:13:09.570
condemned this adventure, secretly hoped for
it to succeed.

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:12.510
In reality, Cavour did something more than
just hope.

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:16.730
The Mastermind from Turin had arranged for
some Piedmontese officers and soldiers to

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:17.870
join the Thousands.

00:13:17.870 --> 00:13:22.750
Officially, they were deserters, but this
was just a ploy to sustain plausible deniability.

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:27.090
He then arranged for a garrison commander
in Tuscany to provide Garibaldi with weapons

00:13:27.090 --> 00:13:28.090
and ammunition.

00:13:28.090 --> 00:13:30.670
Again, the official version was that these
were stolen.

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:33.060
Oh, and the requisitioned steamers?

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:36.270
They were also a covert donation from the
Piedmontese government.

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:40.779
Cavour had also arranged secret meetings with
British Freemasons, as a conduit to secure

00:13:40.779 --> 00:13:43.860
London’s approval and support of the whole
operation.

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:48.610
The British Empire had an economic interest
in causing the demise of the Bourbon Kingdom:

00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:51.150
gaining control of the sulphur mines in Sicily.

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:55.800
On the 11th of May 1860, Garibaldi and his
Thousand landed in Marsala, western Sicily.

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:58.730
The harbour defences responded with a weak
bombardment.

00:13:58.730 --> 00:14:03.520
This was to avoid harming the two neutral
British vessels which “by total coincidence”

00:14:03.520 --> 00:14:06.600
were in the port to stock up on Marsala wine.

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:11.310
On the 15th of May Garibaldi’s redshirts
had their first pitch battle against the Neapolitan

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:12.830
troops, in [Calatafimi].

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:17.570
The Bourbons had gained the upper ground and
the Thousand had to fight an uphill struggle,

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:20.540
literally, with no artillery or cavalry support.

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:23.980
Garibaldi’s second in command, [Nino Bixio],
suggested a retreat.

00:14:23.980 --> 00:14:28.120
To which the General replied:
“Today either we make Italy, or we die!”

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:30.400
And was immediately hit by a rock.

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:31.970
That did not kill his moment, though.

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:35.670
Garibaldi interpreted this as the Neapolitans
being out of ammo!

00:14:35.670 --> 00:14:39.350
So he charged headlong up the Calatafimi hill,
followed by his men.

00:14:39.350 --> 00:14:44.410
The enemies were routed and the Thousand soon
became Thousands, as Sicilian volunteers joined

00:14:44.410 --> 00:14:45.410
their ranks.

00:14:45.410 --> 00:14:48.100
On the 20th of June the redshirts conquered
Palermo.

00:14:48.100 --> 00:14:52.050
Alexandre Dumas, writer of “The Three Musketeers”
at this point joined the Thousand!

00:14:52.050 --> 00:14:55.900
He would later co-author and edit the General’s
autobiography.

00:14:55.900 --> 00:15:00.620
By mid-July the last Neapolitan Division in
Sicily was preparing for a siege in [Messina],

00:15:00.620 --> 00:15:02.510
opposite the ‘tip of the boot’.

00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:07.160
As Garibaldi approached the city, the Bourbon
commander sent a detachment of 2500 men to

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:10.000
block his way in the fortified town of [Milazzo].

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:14.070
Things got sticky, as the enemy commander
played a “Garibaldi” on Garibaldi.

00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:18.290
That his: he stalled the attack with well-placed
sniper and artillery fire from behind the

00:15:18.290 --> 00:15:23.770
walls of Milazzo, and then launched a sudden
flanking counterattack – but with cavalry.

00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:28.460
The cavalry charge surrounded Garibaldi and
his staff and almost succeeded in capturing

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:33.350
him, but the General, saber drawn, cut and
slashed his way out of the encirclement.

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:37.980
Milazzo and Messina fell, and by August the
redshirts had reached the mainland.

00:15:37.980 --> 00:15:43.490
As they marched upwards, Neapolitan resistance
melted away, marred by poor morale and possibly

00:15:43.490 --> 00:15:45.852
by Cavour’s tactic of bribing some of their
generals.

00:15:45.852 --> 00:15:50.900
On the 1st of October the Bourbons staged
their last resistance with the Battle of the

00:15:50.900 --> 00:15:53.450
Volturno river, the largest of Garibaldi’s
career.

00:15:53.450 --> 00:15:56.270
[Vole - tour - no]
Also in this occasion he risked capture or

00:15:56.270 --> 00:16:00.610
death, as his carriage was riddled with gunfire
by a Neapolitan patrol.

00:16:00.610 --> 00:16:05.130
Also in this occasion the General drew his
saber and hacked at the assailants, before

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:06.350
re-joining the main battle.

00:16:06.350 --> 00:16:08.580
The battle was another victory for the redshirts.

00:16:08.580 --> 00:16:12.529
In the meanwhile, the Piedmontese army had
descended from the North, conquering the Eastern

00:16:12.529 --> 00:16:15.150
side of the State of Church in central Italy.

00:16:15.150 --> 00:16:20.220
On the 15th of October, the Piedmontese Army,
led by Victor Emmanuel himself, met with Garibaldi’s

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:22.160
forces in a town called [Teano].

00:16:22.160 --> 00:16:26.400
This was Cavour’s idea of course: the plan
was to join up Garibaldi’s conquests with

00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:28.339
Northern Italy to achieve unification.

00:16:28.339 --> 00:16:32.400
And at the same time, prevent Garibaldi from
attacking Rome, and the French garrison still

00:16:32.400 --> 00:16:33.410
protecting the Pope.

00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:37.900
Cavour was expecting a violent clash, but
it took only a veiled threat from the King

00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:40.680
to avoid that:
“Your troops are tired, General.

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:41.920
Mine are fresh.

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:43.490
It is my turn now.”

00:16:43.490 --> 00:16:46.130
And Garibaldi handed over his conquests to
the King.

00:16:46.130 --> 00:16:50.180
On the 17th of March 1861 the Kingdom of Italy
was finally declared.

00:16:50.180 --> 00:16:54.279
The unification was not an easy process: some
Southern Italians did not take kindly to the

00:16:54.279 --> 00:16:58.240
“Piedmont-isation”
Of their lands and launched a 10 year civil

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:03.400
war, which was for decades covered up as a
series of large scale police operations against

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:04.970
bandits or brigands.

00:17:04.970 --> 00:17:07.110
But that’s another story.

00:17:07.110 --> 00:17:11.360
Back to November 1860 - on the 9th Garibaldi
set sail for Caprera once again.

00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:15.419
As his vessel exited the harbour, British
warships fired a salvo in salute to the man

00:17:15.419 --> 00:17:18.529
who had risked more than anyone to unify Italy.

00:17:18.529 --> 00:17:22.399
The guns of the Piedmontese fleet remained
silent.

00:17:22.399 --> 00:17:29.590
The Ageing General
Giuseppe Garibaldi resumed a simple life in

00:17:29.590 --> 00:17:30.990
his beloved Caprera.

00:17:30.990 --> 00:17:35.280
Like Cincinnatus, the ancient Roman general,
he was content with a few simple supplies

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:39.610
as a reward: stockfish, seeds, and a large
box of pasta.

00:17:39.610 --> 00:17:42.790
His exploits in Southern Italy did not go
unnoticed in the US.

00:17:42.790 --> 00:17:46.950
On the 3rd of December 1860, abolitionist
Frederick Douglass declared in a speech that,

00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:49.879
should the South secede:
“I believe a Garibaldi would arise who would

00:17:49.879 --> 00:17:53.950
march into those States with a thousand men,
and summon to his standard sixty thousand,

00:17:53.950 --> 00:17:56.909
if necessary, to accomplish the freedom of
the slave.”

00:17:56.909 --> 00:18:00.769
This may have been an inspiration for Abrahm
Lincoln, who in July 1861, offered General

00:18:00.769 --> 00:18:03.080
Garibaldi a commission in the Union Army.

00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:07.289
It has been evaluated that his aggressive
tactics may have led to a quicker end to the

00:18:07.289 --> 00:18:09.600
Civil War, but of course this did not happen.

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:13.820
Garibaldi was keen to accept, on the condition
that the abolition of slavery was formalised

00:18:13.820 --> 00:18:16.059
as key war aim for the Union.

00:18:16.059 --> 00:18:20.909
Even if Lincoln agreed in principle, he was
not ready at that stage to make such a declaration

00:18:20.909 --> 00:18:23.360
and Garibaldi eventually declined.

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:28.480
Aged 55, riddled with old wounds and rheumatism,
the General still had some fight left within.

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:33.990
In 1862 he assembled another army to capture
Rome, but he was stopped by the regular Italian

00:18:33.990 --> 00:18:34.990
Army.

00:18:34.990 --> 00:18:39.029
Garibaldi ordered his men not to shoot against
fellow Italians, but a skirmish ensued and

00:18:39.029 --> 00:18:40.539
he was shot in the foot.

00:18:40.539 --> 00:18:42.789
The defeat did not dent his popularity.

00:18:42.789 --> 00:18:46.220
In fact, in April 1864 he visited London,
where he was welcomed triumphantly.

00:18:46.220 --> 00:18:49.549
A journalist at the time wrote
“Respect for truth renders it necessary

00:18:49.549 --> 00:18:54.889
to say that the female portion of those present
were even more eager than their male companions

00:18:54.889 --> 00:18:57.119
to reach Garibaldi”
How Victorian.

00:18:57.119 --> 00:19:01.889
In 1866, the young Kingdom of Italy sided
with Prussia against Austria in what was called

00:19:01.889 --> 00:19:05.539
the 6 weeks war, aka the Third War of Italian
Independence.

00:19:05.539 --> 00:19:10.840
Despite the embarrassment of the 1862 debacle,
Garibaldi agreed to take charge again of the

00:19:10.840 --> 00:19:11.999
Hunters of the Alps.

00:19:11.999 --> 00:19:16.769
The Italian Army made little progress on land
losing at Custoza, while its Navy suffered

00:19:16.769 --> 00:19:18.519
a crushing defeat at Lissa.

00:19:18.519 --> 00:19:23.090
Only the Prussian allies and the Hunters scored
victories on the Austrians.

00:19:23.090 --> 00:19:27.669
In a repeat of the previous war of independence,
Garibaldi’s men were about to seize Trentino,

00:19:27.669 --> 00:19:29.269
when he received an order to halt.

00:19:29.269 --> 00:19:32.909
The Austrians had signed an armistice and
handed over the Venetia region.

00:19:32.909 --> 00:19:36.640
The General replied to the order with a single
word telegram:

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:39.010
“Obbedisco”
“I obey”

00:19:39.010 --> 00:19:43.900
Trentino would be contested for 50 years and
was annexed by Italy only after WWI.

00:19:43.900 --> 00:19:48.889
The following year, now 60, Garibaldi clearly
thought some more fighting would do good to

00:19:48.889 --> 00:19:50.139
his aching joints.

00:19:50.139 --> 00:19:51.139
His target?

00:19:51.139 --> 00:19:52.240
Rome again!

00:19:52.240 --> 00:19:56.730
This time the Italian army did not manage
to intercept him, but he and his men clashed

00:19:56.730 --> 00:19:58.240
with the French near Rome.

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:01.649
The French Army was equipped with the new
Chassepots rifles: these guaranteed a much

00:20:01.649 --> 00:20:06.789
higher rate of fire and quicker reload, making
the bayonet charges of the redshirts futile.

00:20:06.789 --> 00:20:08.710
The expedition was a disaster.

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:10.499
Once again Garibaldi retreated to his Island.

00:20:10.499 --> 00:20:15.140
It looked as though he would retire … but
1870 brought along another occasion for a

00:20:15.140 --> 00:20:16.249
righteous fight.

00:20:16.249 --> 00:20:20.809
Towards the end of the Franco-Prussian war,
Napoleon III’s rule had collapsed and the

00:20:20.809 --> 00:20:24.960
Empire had been replaced by the Paris Commune
and the so called III Republic.

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:29.190
With no ill will against the French, the old
General would fight alongside the young Republic.

00:20:29.190 --> 00:20:32.640
He corralled an army of volunteers and engaged
the Prussians in the Vosges region.

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:36.600
Amidst the catastrophe of the Franco-Prussian
war, the Army of the Vosges was one of the

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:41.990
few French units capable of scoring victories
against the invaders, but it was too little,

00:20:41.990 --> 00:20:42.990
too late.

00:20:42.990 --> 00:20:45.200
The French defeat had a silver lining for
the Italians.

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:51.120
In September of 1870 the garrison protecting
Rome was recalled back to serve in France.

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:54.330
Taking advantage, Italian troops easily marched
into the Eternal City.

00:20:54.330 --> 00:21:02.309
Save for Trentino and the region of Trieste,
Italy as we know it today was finally unified.

00:21:02.309 --> 00:21:07.869
Legacy
After years of battles, adventures, victories

00:21:07.869 --> 00:21:10.299
and defeats, General Garibaldi had finally
settled.

00:21:10.299 --> 00:21:14.090
He spent his retirement years in Caprera,
tending to his land.

00:21:14.090 --> 00:21:15.850
He received tons of fan mail.

00:21:15.850 --> 00:21:19.659
Although it was not stamped, so he had to
pay hundreds of liras in fines!

00:21:19.659 --> 00:21:23.149
In 1880 he married for the third time, with
Francesca Armosino.

00:21:23.149 --> 00:21:27.370
In his last years Garibaldi founded the “League
of Democracy” which advocated universal

00:21:27.370 --> 00:21:32.239
suffrage, female emancipation, the abolition
of ecclesiastical property and a standing

00:21:32.239 --> 00:21:33.869
army for national unity.

00:21:33.869 --> 00:21:38.010
He also voiced his support for the creation
of a European federation, which would be led

00:21:38.010 --> 00:21:40.350
in the future by a greater Germany.

00:21:40.350 --> 00:21:44.149
Garibaldi died on 2 June 1882, at the age
of 75.

00:21:44.149 --> 00:21:48.429
He wished to have a simple cremation, though
he was buried by his farm on the island of

00:21:48.429 --> 00:21:49.490
Caprera.

00:21:49.490 --> 00:21:54.249
Giuseppe Garibaldi is nowadays regarded by
most Italians – and non-Italians – as

00:21:54.249 --> 00:21:58.750
a selfless hero, a brilliant military leader,
the true driving force behind the unification

00:21:58.750 --> 00:22:01.749
of Italy, as well as many other liberal causes.

00:22:01.749 --> 00:22:05.389
However all heroes are men and no man is without
blemish.

00:22:05.389 --> 00:22:10.059
Some revisionist historians argue that his
exploits in Southern Italy were just a colonial

00:22:10.059 --> 00:22:13.029
war disguised by lofty ideals.

00:22:13.029 --> 00:22:18.049
Garibaldi had in fact promised a land reform
to the Sicilian peasants, but did not act

00:22:18.049 --> 00:22:19.049
on it.

00:22:19.049 --> 00:22:23.499
Instead he had ordered a violent repression
of a peasant revolt, to protect British interests.

00:22:23.499 --> 00:22:28.460
At best, he had been an unwitting puppet in
the hands of Cavour, the British and the Freemasons,

00:22:28.460 --> 00:22:34.769
one who had caused the destruction of the
South and the violence of the 1861-1871 civil

00:22:34.769 --> 00:22:35.869
war.

00:22:35.869 --> 00:22:40.440
Other sources claim that in the years spent
sailing the Pacific, he had let go of his

00:22:40.440 --> 00:22:44.440
ideals, participating in the trade of Chinese
slaves for the guano industry.

00:22:44.440 --> 00:22:48.739
According to this version, this is how he
raised the funds to purchase Caprera, not

00:22:48.739 --> 00:22:49.899
through an inheritance.

00:22:49.899 --> 00:22:52.389
The truth, as in many cases, is open to interpretation.

00:22:52.389 --> 00:22:57.730
And even 150 years after the events, politics
can influence the way facts are interpreted.

00:22:57.730 --> 00:23:02.289
But whatever the truth, or lack of truth,
Garibaldi’s achievements are undeniable:

00:23:02.289 --> 00:23:07.739
this is a man who fought and risked his life
well into his sixties, a man who allowed progressive

00:23:07.739 --> 00:23:28.759
ideals to flourish, a man who united a Country.

